+  "draft_pages": {
+    "software-delivery-planning/domain.md": "# Theme: Software delivery planning\n\n## Intent\nThis domain turns a business intent into work that agents can build and that a check from the outside can prove. It serves the product owner, who names the work and approves its design, and the plays that build from that design. Its job comes before the build: it shapes one slice that a person can test end to end, it plans that slice as work items in the tracker, and it writes the eval that says if the intent is met. It does not build, check or release the work, and it never lets a builder read the eval.\n\n> To be deepened later: written by /shape-slice for bi-788 at the directional depth. /discover-domains or /discover-capabilities raises it to the detailed depth.\n\n## Sources\n- the Shape Up author, Shape Up: Stop Running in Circles and Ship Work that Matters (Basecamp, 2019, basecamp.com/shapeup): the field names the work done before a build 'shaping', and keeps it apart from building.\n- the User Story Mapping author, User Story Mapping (O'Reilly, 2014): the field cuts work into thin vertical slices that a person can use and test end to end, then orders them.\n- the Specification by Example author, Specification by Example (Manning, 2011): the field agrees the acceptance examples before the build, and checks the built work against them from the outside.\n- Atlassian Jira issue hierarchy (support.atlassian.com, issue types: epic, story, subtask): the field tracks planned work as an epic that holds stories.\n- docs/adr/049-a-slice-is-shaped-then-planned-then-given-its-eval.md, Decision 1: 'the shaping work is three plays in a row: shape the slice, build the plan, build the eval.'\n- docs/philosophy/idsd.md, IDSD in One Page: the Shape loop, 'the product is ready to build in slices'; and 'The Plan Is the Human's Interface'.\n- docs/philosophy/garura-reference-implementation.md, The Builder/Validator Barrier: the evals are kept away from the builders.\n- Memory (phoenix) knowledge/domains/*.md, the ten shelves: no shelf has a trigger for software delivery work (placement-routing.json, unmatched).\n- Note: the four outside sources are named from the published works; this run did not fetch them. A person checks them before the page is raised to detailed.\n",
+    "capabilities/cap-shape-slice/capability.md": "# Capability: Shape a slice\n\n## Directional intent\nShape the one slice that shows a business intent is served. A slice is an ordered group of features, from any capabilities, that a person can test end to end on one surface. This capability picks those features in build order, the one surface, and the person who tests it. It reads the product model and memory first, and asks the person only what neither answers. It writes the slice and one link, \"slice serves business intent\", and nothing else: the plan, the epic, the stories and the eval are the work of other capabilities. It matters because every later step plans and checks this slice; a slice with no surface a person can test cannot be checked.\n\n> To be deepened later: written by /shape-slice for bi-788 at the directional depth. /discover-domains or /discover-capabilities raises it to the detailed depth.\n\n## Agent intent (ICE)\n> Thin ICE: proposed by /shape-slice for bi-788 (#844, decision 7); deepen with /discover-capabilities.\n- Goal: A business intent has one slice that one person can test end to end on one surface, with its features in build order.\n- Goal: The slice's hidden file holds each feature's failure conditions and its capability's, with exactly one recovery for each.\n- Limit: Write only the slice, its link \"slice serves business intent\", its record, the model parts the slice lacks (marked to be deepened), and the hidden file. Never a plan, an epic, a story or an eval.\n- Limit: Ask the person only what the model and memory do not answer, within the question tier.\n- Fails if: the slice has no surface that a person can test, has no tester, or has more than one surface.\n- Fails if: the person is asked a question that the model or memory answers.\n- Fails if: a \"Fails if\" line of a slice feature or of its capability is missing from the hidden file.\n- Context: serves the product owner (names the intent, answers, approves) and the plays after it, /shape-plan and /shape-eval, which read the slice; reads the intent page, the spine, the ontology (Slice kind) and memory; writes the spine's slices list, product-os/slices/<id>.yaml and .garura/project/plans/<plan-id>/evals/<slug>/failure-and-recovery.md; out are the plan, the epic, the stories and the eval.\n- Checked by: /shape-slice run on a real business intent writes one slice and its hidden file, and no plan, epic, story or eval.\n\n## Sources\n- docs/adr/049-a-slice-is-shaped-then-planned-then-given-its-eval.md, Decision 1.1.\n- docs/adr/048-the-product-model-and-the-work-are-two-layers.md, Decision 2: the slice definition.\n- the User Story Mapping author, User Story Mapping (O'Reilly, 2014): thin vertical slices.\n- GitHub #795 (closed).\n",
+    "capabilities/cap-shape-eval/capability.md": "# Capability: Write the hidden eval\n\n## Directional intent\nWrite the eval that checks, from the outside, that a business intent is met on its slice's surface. The eval is written before the build, from what the plan wrote: the epic, its stories and the slice's hidden file. It never reads an ICE page itself, so the ICE has only one reader. The eval is kept in the plan's evals folder, and no builder, no skill that builds the software and no context sub-agent reads that folder. It matters because a builder that sees the test can build to the test, and then a pass proves nothing.\n\n> To be deepened later: written by /shape-slice for bi-788 at the directional depth. /discover-domains or /discover-capabilities raises it to the detailed depth.\n\n## Agent intent (ICE)\n> Thin ICE: proposed by /shape-slice for bi-788 (#844, decision 7); deepen with /discover-capabilities.\n- Goal: Each slice has one eval that says, from the outside, if its business intent is met on the slice's surface.\n- Limit: Read the epic, its stories and the hidden file; never an ICE page, the usage guide or the code.\n- Limit: Write only in the plan's evals folder: .garura/project/plans/<plan-id>/evals/<slug>/.\n- Fails if: a builder can read the eval.\n- Fails if: the eval checks something the business intent does not ask for.\n- Fails if: the eval play reads an ICE page, the usage guide or the code.\n- Context: serves the product owner, who wants proof that the intent is met, and the running eval after the build; reads what /shape-plan and /shape-slice wrote; until #828 only a read rule keeps the folder from builders; out are running the eval and encrypting it.\n- Checked by: /shape-eval run after /shape-plan writes the eval in the plan's evals folder, and no build play's inputs name that folder.\n\n## Sources\n- docs/adr/049-a-slice-is-shaped-then-planned-then-given-its-eval.md, Decisions 1.3, 3 and 4.\n- docs/philosophy/garura-reference-implementation.md, The Builder/Validator Barrier.\n- the Specification by Example author, Specification by Example (Manning, 2011).\n- GitHub #798 (open).\n",
+    "capabilities/cap-718/capability.md": "# Capability: Build a plan\n\n## Directional intent\nfrom a business intent, build the whole plan — Business Intent → Capability → Feature → Spike/Chore/Bug — as a plan file in the repo, with a tracker issue for every node.\n\nAdded by /shape-slice for bi-788 (ontology v9 and v10, ADR 049): a business intent has one plan, and never a second; the plan's ID is the intent's issue number. The plan holds every slice that serves its intent, found from the link \"slice serves business intent\", and it takes the slice that was shaped; it does not pick or test slices. For each slice it makes one epic, or uses the one that exists, and one story for each feature of the slice. A story's scope of work holds the goal, the limits and the expectations from its feature's ICE and that feature's capability's ICE, and its Context guidance is its own property. A story never holds a failure condition. The plan play does not write the slice's hidden file; /shape-slice writes it (decision 6 of #844, which changes ADR 049 Decisions 1.2 and 4). Note: under ontology v7 a capability and a feature are never tracker issues, so the first paragraph's \"a tracker issue for every node\" is out of date; it is kept until a person agrees the change.\n\n> To be deepened later: written by /shape-slice for bi-788 at the directional depth. /discover-domains or /discover-capabilities raises it to the detailed depth.\n\n## Agent intent (ICE)\n- Goal: Given a business intent, a plan file exists with every capability and feature named, each with its ICE and its tracker issue.\n- Limit: It never waits for a full product model; missing model pages are written as light pages on the fly.\n- Fails if: a node in the plan has no tracker issue, or building stops because a domain or model page is missing.\n- Checked by: building the plans for #717 and its partner gives the same trees that were filed by hand on 2026-10-21.\n\nAdded by /shape-slice for bi-788 (the lines above are kept as they are):\n> Thin ICE: proposed by /shape-slice for bi-788 (#844, decision 7); deepen with /discover-capabilities.\n- Goal: A business intent has exactly one plan, and the plan holds one epic for each slice that serves the intent and one story for each feature of that slice.\n- Limit: The plan does not pick, rank or test slices; the person chose the slice at /shape-slice.\n- Limit: The plan play writes only the plan file, the epic and the stories; the slice's hidden file and its failure and recovery conditions are /shape-slice's (decision 6).\n- Fails if: a story holds a failure condition, or a second plan is made for one business intent.\n- Fails if: the plan play writes a failure condition, a recovery condition or the eval into the plan's evals folder.\n- Context: serves the product owner, who runs and tracks the plan; reads the slice and the two ICE of each story once; writes .garura/project/plans/<intent>.yaml and the tracker's epic and stories; out are picking slices, the hidden file and the eval.\n- Checked by: /shape-plan run after /shape-slice on a real business intent writes the plan file, one epic and one story for each feature of the slice.\n\n## Sources\n- docs/adr/049-a-slice-is-shaped-then-planned-then-given-its-eval.md, Decisions 1.2, 2 and 4.\n- docs/adr/048-the-product-model-and-the-work-are-two-layers.md, Decision 1.\n- GitHub #818 (open).\n",
+    "features/fn-795/feature.md": "# Feature: /shape-slice writes the slice only\n\n## What it does\n/shape-slice <intent> reads the business intent and the product model, asks the person only what the model and memory do not answer, and writes one slice into the spine with its link to the intent. It writes no plan, no epic, no story and no eval. Built in #795. Not built yet: it must also write the slice's hidden file (failure-and-recovery.md) in the plan's evals folder (decision 6 of #844); that needs a change to the play's intent through /edit-play.\n\n> To be deepened later: written by /shape-slice for bi-788 at the directional depth. /discover-domains or /discover-capabilities raises it to the detailed depth.\n\n## Agent intent (ICE)\n> Thin ICE: proposed by /shape-slice for bi-788 (#844, decision 7); deepen with /discover-capabilities.\n- Goal: After /shape-slice <intent>, the spine holds one slice that serves the business intent, with its features in build order, its one surface and its tester.\n- Goal: After /shape-slice <intent>, the plan's evals folder holds the slice's hidden file, with one entry for each feature of the slice. (Not built yet: decision 6 adds it.)\n- Limit: Write no plan, epic, story or eval. The hidden file goes only in .garura/project/plans/<plan-id>/evals/<slug>/, and the confirmation the person sees shows the slice only.\n- Fails if: /shape-slice writes a plan, an epic, a story or an eval.\n- Fails if: a new slice is made for an intent that a slice already serves.\n- Fails if: the hidden file is not written, or a failure condition in it has no recovery or more than one.\n- Context: serves the product owner, who runs it in Claude Code; built in #795 (core/components/plays/shape-slice/): it reads the intent from the model and writes the intent page from the issue when the model has none, asks within the tier, reuses a slice that serves, fills the model, writes the slice and its record, and stops at a typed yes / change / stop.\n- Checked by: the product owner runs /shape-slice 788 in Claude Code and finds one slice in the spine, the hidden file in plans/788/evals/<slug>/, and no other new file outside the model.\n",
+    "features/fn-818/feature.md": "# Feature: /shape-plan writes the plan, the epic and the stories\n\n## What it does\n/shape-plan <intent> runs after /shape-slice, on its own. It reads the slice that serves the intent and the ICE of each feature and its capability, once. It writes the intent's one plan file, one epic for the slice, and one story for each feature. It never writes a failure or recovery condition: the slice's hidden file is /shape-slice's (decision 6 of #844). Not built yet (#818).\n\n> To be deepened later: written by /shape-slice for bi-788 at the directional depth. /discover-domains or /discover-capabilities raises it to the detailed depth.\n\n## Agent intent (ICE)\n> Thin ICE: proposed by /shape-slice for bi-788 (#844, decision 7); deepen with /discover-capabilities.\n- Goal: After /shape-plan <intent>, the intent's one plan file (.garura/project/plans/<intent>.yaml), one epic for the slice and one story for each feature of the slice exist.\n- Goal: A second run finds the plan, the epic and the stories that exist, and makes no copies.\n- Limit: Write only the plan file, the epic and the stories. Never a failure condition, a recovery condition, the hidden file's failure or recovery lines, or the eval.\n- Limit: Each story holds the goal, the limits, the expectations and the Context guidance from its feature's ICE and that feature's capability's ICE.\n- Limit: Nothing goes to the tracker before the product owner's yes.\n- Fails if: a feature of the slice has no story.\n- Fails if: a story or the plan file holds a failure condition or a recovery condition.\n- Fails if: a second run makes a second epic for the slice or a second story for one feature.\n- Context: serves the product owner, who runs and tracks the plan, and the build play, which reads the stories; reads the slice from the spine and the ICE of each feature and its capability; writes .garura/project/plans/ and the tracker; out are the slice, the hidden file, the eval and the build. Not built yet (#818).\n- Checked by: the product owner runs /shape-plan 788 in Claude Code after /shape-slice and finds the plan file, one epic and one story for each feature, with no failure line in any story.\n",
+    "features/fn-798/feature.md": "# Feature: /shape-eval writes the hidden eval\n\n## What it does\n/shape-eval <intent> runs after /shape-plan, on its own. It reads the epic, its stories and the slice's hidden file, and writes the eval for the business intent in the plan's evals folder (.garura/project/plans/<plan-id>/evals/<slug>/). No builder reads that folder. Not built yet (#798).\n\n> To be deepened later: written by /shape-slice for bi-788 at the directional depth. /discover-domains or /discover-capabilities raises it to the detailed depth.\n\n## Agent intent (ICE)\n> Thin ICE: proposed by /shape-slice for bi-788 (#844, decision 7); deepen with /discover-capabilities.\n- Goal: After /shape-eval <intent>, the slice's evals folder holds one eval that checks from the outside, on the slice's surface, each goal and expectation of the stories, and that no failure condition in the hidden file happens.\n- Limit: Read only the epic, its stories and the slice's hidden file; never an ICE page, the usage guide or the code.\n- Limit: Write only in .garura/project/plans/<plan-id>/evals/<slug>/.\n- Fails if: the eval is written outside the plan's evals folder, or a builder's inputs name that folder.\n- Fails if: a story's goal or expectation, or a failure condition in the hidden file, has no check, and the eval does not say that it cannot test it yet.\n- Fails if: a handoff plan that the eval writes names a failure condition, its id or the check.\n- Context: serves the product owner; for Garura the surface is often a play, so the eval runs the play on a test project; the running eval reaches the slice only through its usage guide (guides/<slug>.md). Not built yet (#798).\n- Checked by: the product owner runs /shape-eval 788 in Claude Code after /shape-plan and finds the eval in plans/788/evals/<slug>/, and no build play's inputs name that folder.\n"
+  },
