 without a domain\".\n- Shelf: none. No domain shelf holds the product model of a framework itself (routing for #723).\n\n## Agent intent (ICE)\n- Goal: A plan can build from capabilities and features that have no domain yet.\n- Goal: The ready answer for a node depends only on its confirmed intents and its open needs.\n- Limit: No change to how pages are saved.\n- Limit: The check stops work on a node, never the write that created it.\n- Fails if: the ready check says \"no domain yet\" for a node that serves a confirmed business intent.\n- Fails if: a node with no confirmed business intent, or with an open need, is reported ready.\n- Context: serves the plays that plan or build from ICE (build-plan, /discover-capabilities, /start-change) and the plan owner; touches check_ice_workable.py (both copies), its tests, the spine and the intent pages; in is the ready answer, out are the link-target check and the domain writes.\n- Checked by: the ready check's tests: no domain + confirmed intent = ready; no confirmed intent = not ready; an open need = not ready.\n- Checked by: running the check on Garura's own model reports cap-723 and its features workable with only a no-domain warning.\n- Quality need: the same spine and intent pages always give the same ready answer, with no network or model call."}}}], "from_update": null}
++{"citation_id": "cite-20261024-011", "issue": 844, "play": "shape-slice", "question": "Which part writes the hidden file for slice sl-intent-to-design, and how thin may the ICE of the new capabilities and features be?", "answer": "/shape-slice writes the hidden file; /shape-plan writes only the plan file, the epic and the stories. For cap-shape-slice, cap-shape-eval, fn-795, fn-818 and fn-798 /shape-slice proposes a thin ICE (goals, limits, fails-if lines, context, a check), marked to be deepened with /discover-capabilities; cap-718 keeps its four lines and gains thin lines.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-24T04:42:17Z", "trace": [{"asked": "the product owner decisions on the hidden file and on thin ICE", "found": "decision 6: the hidden file moves to /shape-slice and /shape-plan only plans; decision 7: a thin ICE with at least a goal, limits and one fails-if line, with a warning and advice to run /discover-capabilities", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "844/context/decisions.md", "start": 8, "end": 9, "fingerprint": "sha256:c0095920435c5b6bc44f732d7bd6df4155710bb3439e92a5d4662a349b9d7e98", "text": "6. **The hidden file moves to /shape-slice.** /shape-plan only plans (plan file, epic, stories). /shape-slice writes the slice's hidden failure-and-recovery file. (Changes ontology v10 and ADR 049, which say the plan play writes it.)\n7. **Option C — thin ICE.** For a new capability or feature, /shape-slice proposes a thin ICE itself (at least a goal, limits and one \"fails if\" line), and writes the hidden file from it. It warns that thin ICE leads to problems later and advises /discover-capabilities; it never forces the person to go back. (The ontology already allows work from an ICE that is \"partly ready\".)", "version": null, "around": {"start": 5, "end": 9, "text": "3. **Left out:** eval encryption (#828), the author-slice skill (#838).\n4. **The view.** The slice is shown as: what you run, who tests it, features grouped under capability with each capability linked to its domain, and what is left out. One confirmation, yes or change — not a list of questions first.\n5. **The model.** Reuse what exists, fill what exists in part, add what is missing (domains and capabilities), with research.\n6. **The hidden file moves to /shape-slice.** /shape-plan only plans (plan file, epic, stories). /shape-slice writes the slice's hidden failure-and-recovery file. (Changes ontology v10 and ADR 049, which say the plan play writes it.)\n7. **Option C — thin ICE.** For a new capability or feature, /shape-slice proposes a thin ICE itself (at least a goal, limits and one \"fails if\" line), and writes the hidden file from it. It warns that thin ICE leads to problems later and advises /discover-capabilities; it never forces the person to go back. (The ontology already allows work from an ICE that is \"partly ready\".)"}}}], "from_update": null}
++{"citation_id": "cite-20261024-012", "issue": 844, "play": "shape-slice", "question": "How is each entry of the hidden file for slice sl-intent-to-design written, and who fixes each failure?", "answer": "One recovery for each fails-if line of a feature ICE and its capability ICE (F1 to F18): When is the failure as a symptom, Way back is the inverse as guidance and never code, Who fixes is the builder alone when the fix is mechanical, and a person when it needs judgment, a tracker write or an approval. 10 of 18 are the builder alone, 8 need a person.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-24T04:42:17Z", "trace": [{"asked": "the recovery rule and the handoff rule", "found": "exactly one recovery per failure condition; trigger is the symptom, direction the inverse as guidance never code; autonomous when mechanical, human when it needs judgment, an external mapping, an approval or an outward-facing decision", "source": {"tier": "garura", "path": "core/memory/standards/rules/expectation-generation.md", "start": 33, "end": 60, "fingerprint": "sha256:f2bf0956b467c96692a31b10ff31ca25819dfcab71df69cffd63a2b21d2775c6", "text": "## Recovery — one entry per failure condition\n\nFor every failure condition `F` in the intent, emit exactly one recovery entry:\n\n- `for_failure`: F's id.\n- `trigger`: F restated as an observable symptom (what the validator detects).\n- `direction`: the **inverse of F** — the state that must hold for F not to trip,\n  phrased as directional guidance toward it. Never implementation, never code.\n- `handoff`: routed by the handoff rule below.\n- `derivable_at_l4`: `true` for autonomous entries the runtime can execute without a\n  human; `false` for human entries.\n\n### The handoff rule (autonomous vs human)\n\nThis is the core routing decision, and the seed of `intent-resolver`'s runtime\nrecovery routing and Level 4 autonomous recovery.\n\n- **`autonomous`** when recovering is **mechanical** — a deterministic transform of\n  o