++    "draft": "# Asking a Person\n\nCanonical rule for how a play asks a person a question — at a checkpoint, in a draft the\nperson approves, or in a brief that ends with questions. Every play that asks a person points\nto this file. It does not say *what* to ask; it says how each question is shown.\n\n## The opening\n\nBefore the first question, say in two or three plain sentences what the person decides, and\nwhat happens with their answer. The person must know what is at stake before they read a\nquestion.\n\n## Each question\n\nEach question asks one thing. When a question has an \"and\" in it, make it two questions.\n\nEach question shows four parts, in this order:\n\n1. **Question** — the one thing the person decides, in plain words.\n2. **Answer** — what the model or the proposal gives back: a real answer, in general words.\n   Never \"the place where X is kept\" or \"see the file\". If there is no answer yet, write the\n   proposed answer and say that it is proposed.\n3. **Why it matters** — what goes wrong, or what changes, if the answer is wrong.\n4. **Example** — several examples from different domains, never just one (for example a\n   clinic, a shop and a bank). One example pulls the answer into that one domain.\n\n## Read it back\n\nRead each question back with no context: not the issue, not the earlier questions, not the\nfiles. Ask it only when a stranger could answer it. If a stranger could not, rewrite it.\n\n## Words\n\n- Plain English, in the style of ASD-STE100: short sentences, one idea each, common words.\n- Personas only (for example \"the clinic manager\"). Never a real person's name.\n\n## Checkpoints\n\nA play's checkpoint shows its draft in this format. A draft that does not follow this rule is\nnot ready to show.\n\n## Related\n\n- `rules/user-facing-voice.md` — the register of every reply to the user\n- `templates/approval-prompt.md` — the checkpoint rendering\n",
